The MIT licence covers the CODE ONLY.

data/srd-1.0.json is derived from the Daggerheart System Reference Document
1.0, (c) Critical Role, LLC, and is redistributed under the Darrington Press
Community Gaming License. See the attribution in README.md.

The DPCGL was cited here by bare URL, with no version and no retrieval date.
That is not a citation of a document whose own Section 11 reserves DRP's right
to amend it at any time and without notice: it names whatever is at that
address today, which is not necessarily the licence this release was published
under. The version this release is published under is:

    Darrington Press Community Gaming License, version 2.0
    published 26 August 2026 (the document itself carries "Last Updated
    8/05/2026" on its first page; the publisher's index and the filename both
    say 26 August 2026, and they disagree by three weeks. The publication date
    is cited here and the printed one recorded, because a reader checking this
    against the PDF must find both.)
    https://darringtonpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/DPCGL_2.0_AUG_26_2026.pdf
    retrieved 2026-09-01
    SHA-256 of that PDF:
      f7f62d775a43eecc4ad8f438a233a6aaf92f50396cb412c5ea91c0a988be6eca

    WHY THIS EDITION AND NOT THE PREVIOUS ONE. The 30 July 2025 text listed
    Public Game Content as "Daggerheart System Reference Document 1.0
    (including Domain icons) and Candela Obscura" and did not name SRD 2.0.
    This app ships SRD 2.0. Version 2.0 of the licence, in the same Section
    1.6, names "Daggerheart System Reference Document 2.0 (including Domain
    icons) and Candela Obscura", and adds that SRD 1.0 "remains Public Game
    Content for existing and legacy DRP Games". Its Section 4 attribution
    template is word-for-word the previous one with the version changed.

    Index of versions, and the current one:
    https://darringtonpress.com/license/

The same file said of the fonts that "each licence text sits beside the files
it covers", and it did - while the two licences that cover this project itself
sat nowhere the build could reach. Both now ship:

    src/legal/dpcgl-2026-08-26.txt   the text of the PDF above, extracted
                                     mechanically with `pdftotext -layout`.
                                     A transcription, not the authority: the
                                     PDF is. Compiled into the bundle, so it is
                                     readable in Settings > About with no
                                     network.
    dist/LICENSE.txt                 this file, emitted into every build.
    dist/legal/DPCGL-2026-08-26.txt  the text above, emitted into every build.

The two files under dist/ are for whoever holds a copy of the software; the
copies compiled into the bundle are for whoever is using it offline.

The bundled fonts in public/fonts/ are licensed under the SIL Open Font
License 1.1; each licence text sits beside the files it covers.
